<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-section-CP-Bayes" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Bayes' Theorem</title>

<introduction>
 <p> 
    By completing the problems provided below, you will gain additional practice computing conditional probability. Specifically, you will practice using: 
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Law of Total Probability
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
         Bayes' Theorem
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Tree Diagrams
        </p>
      </li>
 
    </ul>
  </p>
</introduction>

 <exercises xml:id="hw-CP-Bayes" >
   <title>Interactive Exercises</title>



<exercise xml:id="ex-notification-filter-bayes">
    <title>Smart Filter</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            An AI-powered application filters incoming phone notifications into three categories that partition a student's daily alerts: academic alerts (<m>A_1</m>), student club updates (<m>A_2</m>), and promotional spam (<m>A_3</m>). The probabilities for incoming notifications are:
            <me>P(A_1) = 0.3, \quad P(A_2) = 0.1, \quad P(A_3) = 0.6</me>
        
            Let <m>E</m> be the event that a notification triggers an immediate haptic vibration alert. The app's filter settings yield the following conditional probabilities:
            <me>P(E \mid A_1) = 0.3, \quad P(E \mid A_2) = 0.5, \quad P(E \mid A_3) = 0.7</me>
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Part 1: Law of Total Probability (Fill-in-the-blank) -->
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                What is the probability that a randomly arriving notification will trigger a haptic vibration alert? Enter your answer as a decimal value rounded to two decimal places.
            </p>
       <p>
        <m>P(E)</m>=<fillin width="8" answer="0.56"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
   <hint>
            <p>
                Apply the Law of Total Probability! 
            </p>
        </hint>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              To find the total probability of event <m>E</m>, we apply the Law of Total Probability: 
                <md>
                    <mrow>P(E) \amp = P(E \cap A_1) + P(E \cap A_2) + P(E \mid A_3)</mrow>
                    <mrow>
                     \amp = P(E \mid A_1)P(A_1) + P(E \mid A_2)P(A_2) + P(E \mid A_3)P(A_3)</mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = (0.3)(0.3) + (0.5)(0.1) + (0.7)(0.6)</mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = 0.09 + 0.05 + 0.42 = 0.56</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
</evaluate>
</evaluation>
       
    </task>

    <!-- Part 2: Bayes' Theorem (Multiple Choice) -->
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose the student's phone vibrates, what is the  probability that this specific notification came from a student club update?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Apply Bayes' Theorem. You calculated the denominator in part (a).
            </p>
        </hint>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <m>0.089</m> 
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Correct! We compute <m>P(A_2 \mid E)</m> by applying Bayes' Theorem: <me>P(A_2 \mid E)=\frac{P(E \mid A_2)P(A_2)}{P(E)} = \frac{0.05}{0.56} \approx 0.089</me>.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <m>0.500</m>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Incorrect. This is the  conditional probability <m>P(E \mid A_2)</m>. You need to compute <m>P(A_2 \mid E)</m>.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <m>0.100</m>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Incorrect. This is the prior  probability <m>P(A_2)</m>. You need to compute <m>P(A_2 \mid E)</m>. [Note: Because student club alerts are less likely to vibrate than other alerts, knowing that a vibration happened should decrease the probability of <m>A_2</m> slightly.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <m>0.179</m>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Incorrect. You most likely calculated the numerator of Bayes' Theorem incorrectly. Try <m>P(E\cap A_2)=P(E \mid A_2)P(A_2)</m> .
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <m>0.893</m>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Incorrect. You most likely calculated the numerator of Bayes' Theorem incorrectly. Try <m>P(E\cap A_2)=P(E \mid A_2)P(A_2)</m> .
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
        
      <!--  <solution>
            <p>
                To solve for the conditional inverse probability, we employ <term>Bayes' Theorem</term>:
                <me>P(A_2 \mid E) = \frac{P(E \mid A_2)P(A_2)}{P(E)}</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                1. Compute the numerator (the probability that a notification is a Club alert AND triggers a vibration):
                <me>P(E \cap A_2) = P(E \mid A_2)P(A_2) = 0.5 \times 0.1 = 0.05</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                2. Divide by the total probability space <m>P(E) = 0.56</m> evaluated in the previous task:
                <me>P(A_2 \mid E) = \frac{0.05}{0.56} = \frac{5}{56} \approx 0.0893</me>
            </p>
        </solution> -->
    </task>
</exercise>



<exercise xml:id="ex-heart-disease-bayes-matching">
    <title>Gum Disease and Heart Attacks</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Medical researchers have uncovered strong statistical links between oral health and cardiovascular conditions!  <m>83\%</m> of people who have suffered a heart attack had periodontal disease (an inflammation of the gums), while only <m>34\%</m> of heart-healthy people have this disease. 
        </p>
        <p>
            Suppose that in a certain community, heart attacks occur with a probability of <m>15\%</m>. Let <m>H</m> represent the event that an individual has a heart attack, and let <m>G</m> represent the event that an individual has periodontal gum disease.
        </p>
        <p>
            Match each of the probabilities given on the left with their corresponding values on the left. 
        </p>
    </statement>

    <matching>
        <!-- Part 1: Component Identification Given Values -->
  
            <premise ref="h" > <m>P(H)</m></premise>
            <response xml:id="h"><m>0.1500</m></response>

            <premise ref="g_giv_h"><m>P(G \mid H)</m></premise>
            <response xml:id="g_giv_h"><m>0.8300</m></response>

            <premise ref="g_giv_hc"> <m>P(G \mid H^c)</m></premise>
            <response xml:id="g_giv_hc"><m>0.3400</m></response>


        <!-- Part 2: Intermediate and Final Computations -->
            
            <premise ref="g_and_h"><m>P(G \cap H)</m></premise>
            <response xml:id="g_and_h"><m>0.1245</m></response>

            <premise ref="g"><m>P(G)</m></premise>
            <response xml:id="g"><m>0.4135</m></response>

            <premise ref="h_giv_g"> <m>P(H \mid G)</m></premise>
            <response xml:id="h_giv_g"><m>0.3011</m></response>


        <!-- DISTRACTORS (Empty premises create options on the right with no matching pair) -->
  
           
            <response><m>0.0674</m></response>
            <response><m>0.5865</m></response>

    </matching>
    
    <hint>
        <p>
            First identify which probabilities you have been given oin the problem statement. It might be helpful to draw a tree diagram. 
        </p>
    </hint>
    
    <!--<solution>
        <p>
            Let <m>H</m> denote the event that a person has a heart attack, and let <m>G</m> denote the event that a person has periodontal gum disease. From the problem setup, we can pull out our given components directly:
            <ul>
                <li><p><m>P(H) = 0.15 \implies P(H^c) = 1 - 0.15 = 0.85</m></p></li>
                <li><p><m>P(G \mid H) = 0.83</m></p></li>
                <li><p><m>P(G \mid H^c) = 0.34</m></p></li>
            </ul>
        </p>
        <p>
            <bold>Step-by-step Solution for the Remaining Calculations:</bold>
        </p>
        <p>
            1. <term>Find the Total Probability of Gum Disease, <m>P(G)</m>:</term>
            We apply the Law of Total Probability to find the total weighted footprint of the disease across the community:
            <md>
                <mrow>P(G) \amp = P(G \mid H)P(H) + P(G \mid H^c)P(H^c)</mrow>
                <mrow>P(G) \amp = (0.83)(0.15) + (0.34)(0.85)</mrow>
                <mrow>P(G) \amp = 0.1245 + 0.2890 = 0.4135</mrow>
            </md>
            Thus, <m>P(G) = 0.4135</m> (this matches option d from your original list).
        </p>
        <p>
            2. <term>Find the Posterior Conditional Probability, <m>P(H \mid G)</m>:</term>
            Now, we apply Bayes' Theorem to find the probability that a person who is confirmed to have gum disease will go on to have a heart attack:
            <me>P(H \mid G) = \frac{P(G \mid H)P(H)}{P(G)} = \frac{0.1245}{0.4135} \approx 0.301088</me>
            Rounding to four decimal places yields <m>0.3011</m> (which matches option c from your original list).
        </p>
    </solution> -->
</exercise>



<exercise xml:id="ex-employment-drug-test-matching">
    <title>Workplace Drug Testing </title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            A third-party logistics company called 3PL handles  tasks like storage, packing, and shipping for other businesses.
            3PL uses a rapid oral screening test to determine whether machine operators have controlled substances in their systems. 
            </p> 
            <p>
                The rapid screening test is not 100% reliable. It is known that:
                </p>
            <ul>
                <li><p><m>5\%</m> of employees who have <em>not</em> consumed a controlled substance get a false-positive reading.</p></li>

                <li><p><m>10\%</m> of employees who have consumed a controlled substance get a false-negative reading.</p></li>
            </ul>
<p>
            Suppose that <m>17\%</m> of 3PL's machine operators have consumed a controlled substance, and the safety manager tests an employee at random. 
        </p>
        <p>
            Match each conditional probability on the left with its correct calculated value on the right. 
        </p>
    </statement>

    <cardsort>

        <!-- DISTRACTOR 2 (Empty Premise) -->
        <match>
            <response><m>0.8055</m></response>
        </match>

        <!-- Part A Pair -->
        <match>
            <premise>
                Find the probability that the employee has consumed a controlled substance, given that the test reading is positive.
            </premise>
            <response>
                <m>0.7866</m>
            </response>
        </match>

         <!-- DISTRACTOR 3 (Empty Premise) -->
        <match>
            <response><m>0.9500</m></response>
        </match>
   

        <!-- Part B Pair -->
        <match>
            <premise>
                The probability that the employee has not consumed a controlled substance, given that the test reading is negative.
            </premise>
            <response>
                <m>0.9789</m>
            </response>
        </match>

              <!-- DISTRACTOR 1 (Empty Premise) -->
        <match>
            <response><m>0.1945</m></response>
        </match>
 </cardsort>
      

       
    
    <hint>
        <p>
            Let <m>D</m> be the event the employee has consumed a controlled substance (drug), and let <m>P</m> be a positive test result. Now organize the information you have been given in a tree diagram.
        </p>
    </hint>
    
    <!--<solution>
        <p>
            Let <m>D</m> denote the event that the employee has consumed the substance, and let <m>T</m> denote a positive test reading. We are given the following information:
            <me>P(D) = 0.17 \implies P(D^c) = 0.83</me>
            <me>P(T \mid D^c) = 0.05 \implies P(T^c \mid D^c) = 0.95 \quad \text{(False Positive Rate)}</me>
            <me>P(T^c \mid D) = 0.10 \implies P(T \mid D) = 0.90 \quad \text{(False Negative Rate)}</me>
        </p>
        <p>
            <bold>Step-by-step Solution for Each Scenario:</bold>
        </p>
        <p>
            1. <term>Question A: Finding <m>P(D \mid T)</m></term>
            First, find the total probability of a positive test, <m>P(T)</m>, using the Law of Total Probability:
            <md>
                <mrow>P(T) \amp = P(T \mid D)P(D) + P(T \mid D^c)P(D^c)</mrow>
                <mrow>P(T) \amp = (0.90)(0.17) + (0.05)(0.83)</mrow>
                <mrow>P(T) \amp = 0.1530 + 0.0415 = 0.1945</mrow>
            </md>
            Now, apply Bayes' Theorem to calculate the posterior probability:
            <me>P(D \mid T) = \frac{P(T \mid D)P(D)}{P(T)} = \frac{0.1530}{0.1945} \approx 0.78663</me>
        </p>
        <p>
            2. <term>Question B: Finding <m>P(D^c \mid T^c)</m></term>
            First, find the total probability of a negative test, <m>P(T^c)</m>:
            <me>P(T^c) = 1 - P(T) = 1 - 0.1945 = 0.8055</me>
            Now, apply Bayes' Theorem to find the probability of being drug-free given a negative screen:
            <me>P(D^c \mid T^c) = \frac{P(T^c \mid D^c)P(D^c)}{P(T^c)} = \frac{(0.95)(0.83)}{0.8055} = \frac{0.7885}{0.8055} \approx 0.97889</me>
        </p>
    </solution> -->
</exercise>





<exercise xml:id="ex-server-filter-bayes">
    <title>Email Scanner Diagnostics </title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            An automated campus server uses a machine learning algorithm to scan incoming email files. Let <m>M</m> be the event that a file is malicious software, and let <m>T</m> be the event that the scanner flags the file as a threat. The scanner's system analytics provide the following  probabilities:
            <me>P(M) = 0.14, \quad P(T \mid M) = 0.92, \quad P(T^c \mid M^c) = 0.67</me>
            If a randomly arriving file is flagged as a threat by the scanner, what is the probability that it is actually a malicious software file?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
            <statement>
                <m>0.312</m> 
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Correct!  Apply Bayes' Theorem: <me>P(M \mid T) = \frac{0.1288}{0.4126} \approx 0.312</me>.  
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
            <statement>
                <m>0.413</m>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. This value is the total probability that a file gets flagged as a threat by the scanner, <m>P(T)</m>. You must use this number as your denominator in Bayes' Theorem to find the conditional probability <m>P(M \mid T)</m>.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
            <statement>
                <m>0.129</m>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. This value is the probability of the intersection <m>P(M \cap T)</m>. You must use this number as your numerator in Bayes' Theorem to find the conditional probability <m>P(M \mid T)</m>.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
            <statement>
                <m>0.920</m>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. This value is the true positive rate (conditional probability) that is given. You need to find <m>P(M \mid T)</m>.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
            <statement>
                <m>0.688</m>
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. This is <m>P(M^c \mid T)</m>, which is the complement of what you need to find.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
    </choices>
    <!--<hint>
        <p>
            You need to set up Bayes' Theorem: <m>P(A \mid B) = \frac{P(B \mid A)P(A)}{P(B)}</m>. Before calculating, look closely at your given values: you are given <m>P(B^c \mid A^c) = 0.06</m>, which means its conditional complement is <m>P(B \mid A^c) = 1 - 0.06 = 0.94</m>. Use that to find <m>P(B)</m> first.
        </p>
    </hint> -->
   <!-- <solution>
        <p>
            To evaluate the conditional probability <m>P(A \mid B)</m>, we utilize the conditional base rules and apply Bayes' Theorem:
        </p>
        <p>
            1. <term>Find the prior and conditional complements:</term>
            <ul>
                <li><p>The probability that a file is safe (<m>A^c</m>): <m>P(A^c) = 1 - P(A) = 1 - 0.44 = 0.56</m></p></li>
                <li><p>The conditional probability that a safe file is flagged (<m>B \mid A^c</m>): <m>P(B \mid A^c) = 1 - P(B^c \mid A^c) = 1 - 0.06 = 0.94</m></p></li>
            </ul>
        </p>
        <p>
            2. <term>Calculate the total probability space for the denominator, <m>P(B)</m>:</term>
            Using the Law of Total Probability:
            <md>
                <mrow>P(B) \amp = P(B \mid A)P(A) + P(B \mid A^c)P(A^c)</mrow>
                <mrow>P(B) \amp = (0.03)(0.44) + (0.94)(0.56)</mrow>
                <mrow>P(B) \amp = 0.0132 + 0.5264 = 0.5396</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            3. <term>Apply Bayes' Theorem to find the posterior probability:</term>
            <me>P(A \mid B) = \frac{P(B \mid A)P(A)}{P(B)} = \frac{0.0132}{0.5396} \approx 0.02446</me>
            Rounding to four decimal places yields <m>0.0245</m>.
        </p>
    </solution>-->
</exercise>

   </exercises>



<exercises xml:id="hw-CP-Bayes-turn-in" >
   <title>Written Exercises</title>



    
<exercise xml:id="ex-world-cup-bayes-mc">
    <title>World Cup</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Your favorite national football team is competing in the FIFA World Cup. Based on their qualifying record and performance metrics (compiled by sports analytics models), you assign an initial probability of <m>64\%</m> that they will win the entire championship. 
        </p>
        <p>
            Historical tournament records indicate that when a team ultimately wins the World Cup, they win their first group stage match <m>71\%</m> of the time. When they fail to win the championship, they win their first group stage match only <m>20\%</m> of the time. 
        </p>
        <p>
            The opening match is over, and your team has not won.  What is the probability that they will still go on to win the World Cup?
        </p>
    </statement>
   
    <hint>
        <p>
            If you're not sure where to start, then try using the complement rule to find the probability that  the team does not win their first match, given they win the tournament. </p>
            <p>How will this help you compute the desired probability? 
        </p>
    </hint>
    <!--<solution>
        <p>
            We define our events as follows: Let <m>W</m> be the event that the team wins the World Cup championship, and let <m>L</m> be the event that the team loses their first group stage match. We are given:
            <me>P(W) = 0.64 \implies P(W^c) = 1 - 0.64 = 0.36</me>
            <me>P(\text{Win First} \mid W) = 0.71 \implies P(L \mid W) = 1 - 0.71 = 0.29</me>
            <me>P(\text{Win First} \mid W^c) = 0.20 \implies P(L \mid W^c) = 1 - 0.20 = 0.80</me>
        </p>
        <p>
            We want to find the posterior conditional probability <m>P(W \mid L)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            1. <term>Find the overall probability of losing the first match, <m>P(L)</m>:</term>
            Using the Law of Total Probability:
            <md>
                <mrow>P(L) \amp = P(L \mid W)P(W) + P(L \mid W^c)P(W^c)</mrow>
                <mrow>P(L) \amp = (0.29)(0.64) + (0.80)(0.36)</mrow>
                <mrow>P(L) \amp = 0.1856 + 0.2880 = 0.4736</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            2. <term>Apply Bayes' Theorem to calculate the final probability:</term>
            <me>P(W \mid L) = \frac{P(L \mid W)P(W)}{P(L)} = \frac{0.1856}{0.4736} \approx 0.39189</me>
            Rounding to three decimal places matches option (a): <m>0.392</m>.
        </p>
    </solution>-->
</exercise>


    <exercise label="ex-bayes-custom-dice">
        <title>The Weighted Collectible Die Experiment</title>
<introduction>

            <p>
        A tabletop gaming store owner purchases an unverified chest containing a total of 16 identical-looking custom, collectible d20 (20-sided) gaming dice. Within the chest, 11 are authentic factory originals that are perfectly balanced and fair, landing on the maximum critical hit symbol exactly 5% of the time. The remaining 5 dice are counterfeit, weighted so that they land on the critical hit symbol with a probability of <m>1/4</m>.
    </p>

</introduction>
        <!-- Part A: Total Probability Theorem -->
        <task xml:id="task-dice-total-prob">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose the store owner reaches into the chest, picks exactly one die completely at random, and rolls it once. What is the probability that the die lands on the critical hit symbol?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <!--<solution>
                <p>
                    Let <m>F</m> denote the event that a fair factory original die is selected, and let <m>B</m> denote the event that a biased counterfeit die is selected. Their initial baseline selection probabilities are:
                    <me>P(F) = \frac{11}{16} \quad \text{and} \quad P(B) = \frac{5}{16}</me>
                    Let <m>H</m> denote the event that the chosen die lands on the critical hit symbol. According to the <strong>Law of Total Probability</strong>:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>P(H) \amp = P(F) \cdot P(H \mid F) + P(B) \cdot P(H \mid B)</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = \left(\frac{11}{16} \cdot \frac{1}{2}\right) + \left(\frac{5}{16} \cdot \frac{3}{4}\right)</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = \frac{11}{32} + \frac{15}{64}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = \frac{22}{64} + \frac{15}{64} = \frac{37}{64} \approx 0.5781</mrow>
                    </md>
                    The probability that the randomly selected die lands on a critical hit is exactly <strong>37/64</strong> (or approximately 57.81%).
                </p>
            </solution> -->
        </task>

        <!-- Part B: Single-Stage Bayes' Theorem -->
        <task xml:id="task-dice-bayes-single">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose the store owner picks a die completely at random and rolls it once, and it lands on a critical hit. Given this outcome, what is the  probability that the die is one of the counterfeits?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <!--<solution>
                <p>
                    We want to evaluate the posterior probability <m>P(B \mid H)</m>. Applying <strong>Bayes' Theorem</strong> and leveraging our total probability denominator calculated in Part A:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>P(B \mid H) \amp = \frac{P(B) \cdot P(H \mid B)}{P(H)}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = \frac{\frac{5}{16} \cdot \frac{3}{4}}{\frac{37}{64}}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = \frac{\frac{15}{64}}{\frac{37}{64}} = \frac{15}{37} \approx 0.4054</mrow>
                    </md>
                    Given a single critical hit result, the probability that the chosen die is a counterfeit weighted fake increases from its baseline rate of 31.25% up to approximately <strong>40.54%</strong>.
                </p>
            </solution> -->
        </task>

        <!-- Part C: Multi-Stage Sequential Replication Bayes' Theorem -->
        <task xml:id="task-dice-bayes-multi">
            <statement>
                <p>
Suppose the store owner picks a die completely at random and rolls <em>that same exact die</em> three times in a row, and it lands on a critical hit every time. Given this consecutive streak, what is the probability that the dice is one of the counterfeits?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <!--<solution>
                <p>
                    Let <m>H_5</m> denote the event of observing five consecutive critical hits from the same chosen die. Because the five rolls are conditionally independent given the specific die's true identity, we raise the single-trial probabilities to the 5th power:
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Conditional probability if the die is a fair original: <m>P(H_5 \mid F) = \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^5 = \frac{1}{32} = \frac{32}{1024}</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Conditional probability if the die is a biased counterfeit: <m>P(H_5 \mid B) = \left(\frac{3}{4}\right)^5 = \frac{243}{1024}</m></p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p>
                    First, calculate the updated total probability denominator for this sequential replication outcome across the whole chest:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>P(H_5) \amp = P(F) \cdot P(H_5 \mid F) + P(B) \cdot P(H_5 \mid B)</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = \left(\frac{11}{16} \cdot \frac{1}{32}\right) + \left(\frac{5}{16} \cdot \frac{243}{1024}\right)</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = \frac{11}{512} + \frac{1215}{16384} = \frac{352}{16384} + \frac{1215}{16384} = \frac{1567}{16384} \approx 0.0956</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Now apply Bayes' Theorem to find the updated posterior weight:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>P(B \mid H_5) \amp = \frac{P(B) \cdot P(H_5 \mid B)}{P(H_5)}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = \frac{\frac{1215}{16384}}{\frac{1567}{16384}} = \frac{1215}{1567} \approx 0.7754</mrow>
                    </md>
                    Observing five consecutive critical hits from the same die provides massive statistical evidence, causing our confidence that you picked a counterfeit weighted die to jump up to approximately <strong>77.54%</strong>.
                </p>
            </solution>-->
        </task>
    </exercise>



    <exercise label="ex-static-conditional-convention-merch">
        <title>[Review Question] Fandom Convention Analytics</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
        An organizer at a multi-genre pop culture convention conducts an entry poll of attendees. The survey data reveals that 28% of the attendees are wearing apparel from an anime series. Among those wearing anime apparel, exactly 25% are also wearing merchandise from a sci-fi media franchise. Additionally, it is recorded that 35% of all surveyed convention attendees are wearing merchandise from a sci-fi media franchise.
        </p>
    
        </introduction>

        <!-- Part A: Conditional Probability Calculation -->
        <task xml:id="task-static-merch-conditional">
            <statement>
                <p>
                      Suppose an attendee is selected  at random from this convention crowd. What is the  probability that the randomly selected attendee is wearing apparel from an anime series, given that they are wearing merchandise from a sci-fi media franchise?
                </p>
            </statement>
          <!--  <solution>
                <p>
                    First, use the multiplicative multiplication rule to calculate the joint intersection probability of an attendee wearing both items simultaneously (<m>P(\text{Anime} \cap \text{Sci-Fi})</m>):
                    <me>P(\text{Anime} \cap \text{Sci-Fi}) = P(\text{Anime}) \cdot P(\text{Sci-Fi} \mid \text{Anime}) = 0.28 \cdot 0.25 = 0.0700</me>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Now, evaluate the requested conditional probability by restricting the target space to the Sci-Fi attendee universe (<m>P(\text{Sci-Fi}) = 0.35</m>):
                    <me>P(\text{Anime} \mid \text{Sci-Fi}) = \frac{P(\text{Anime} \cap \text{Sci-Fi})}{P(\text{Sci-Fi})} = \frac{0.0700}{0.3500} = 0.2000</me>
                    The conditional probability is exactly <strong>0.2000</strong>.
                </p>
            </solution>-->
        </task>

        <!-- Part B: Dependence and Correlation -->
        <task xml:id="task-static-merch-dependence">
            <statement>
                <p>
                     Determine if the selection of anime apparel and sci-fi media franchise merchandise are independent, positively correlated, or negatively correlated. Justify your reasoning.
                </p>
            </statement>
           <!-- <solution>
                <p>
                    To establish the statistical relationship, compare the conditional probability found in Part A to the unconditional baseline probability:
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Unconditional baseline probability of wearing Anime apparel: <m>P(\text{Anime}) = 0.2800</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Conditional probability of wearing Anime apparel given Sci-Fi gear: <m>P(\text{Anime} \mid \text{Sci-Fi}) = 0.2000</m></p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Because <m>P(\text{Anime} \mid \text{Sci-Fi}) \neq P(\text{Anime})</m>, the two events are not independent. Furthermore, because <m>P(\text{Anime} \mid \text{Sci-Fi}) \lt P(\text{Anime})</m> (meaning the introduction of the condition reduces the likelihood weight), the events are <strong>negatively correlated</strong>. Alternatively, this is verified because the actual intersection (<m>0.0700</m>) is less than the expected product under independence (<m>0.28 \cdot 0.35 = 0.0980</m>).
                </p>
            </solution>-->
        </task>

        <!-- Part C: Probability of Neither Item -->
        <task xml:id="task-static-merch-neither">
            <statement>
                <p>
                     What is the probability that a randomly selected convention attendee is wearing <em>neither</em> anime apparel nor sci-fi media franchise merchandise?
                </p>
            </statement>
           <!-- <solution>
                <p>
                    To find the probability that an attendee wears neither item, we compute the complement of the union of both events: <m>P((\text{Anime} \cup \text{Sci-Fi})^c) = 1 - P(\text{Anime} \cup \text{Sci-Fi})</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    First, apply the general addition rule to calculate the probability that an attendee is wearing at least one of the two types of merchandise:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>P(\text{Anime} \cup \text{Sci-Fi}) \amp = P(\text{Anime}) + P(\text{Sci-Fi}) - P(\text{Anime} \cap \text{Sci-Fi})</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = 0.2800 + 0.3500 - 0.0700 = 0.5600</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Now, apply the complement rule to calculate the remaining area outside of both sets:
                    <me>P((\text{Anime} \cup \text{Sci-Fi})^c) = 1 - 0.5600 = 0.4400</me>
                    The probability that an attendee is wearing neither type of merchandise is exactly <strong>0.4400</strong>.
                </p>
            </solution>-->
        </task>
    </exercise>



